The discovery also provides insight into the evolution of feathers in birds.
Although dinosaur footprints lacked a well-developed heel bone called a "raxis," researchers concluded that, since other features of the foot—such as the ankle and toes—were present, the raxis must have evolved later in the evolutionary process compared to these other features.
The structure of the feathers indicates that they were used not for flight, but for temperature regulation or display purposes. According to researchers, these feathers belonged to a young dinosaur, but the specimen shows adult feathers rather than juvenile ones.
