In France, voting follows a low-tech tradition: voters separate themselves in a booth, place the pre-printed paper ballot showing their chosen candidate into an envelope.
After officials have verified the voter's identity, the voter inserts the ballot into the voting box and signs the ballot.
French electoral legislation strictly codifies these procedures.
Since 1988, the transparency of ballot boxes has been required to ensure that observers and voters can testify that no ballots were present at the beginning of the voting and that no additional ballots are added beyond those properly counted and authorized.
Candidates may send representatives to observe all stages of the process. At night, votes are counted under strict supervision by scrutineers, in accordance with special regulations.
