Ocelots enjoy eating small animals. If possible, they catch monkeys, snakes, rodents, and birds. Nearly all the animals ocelots hunt are much smaller than themselves.
According to scientists, ocelots track and locate their prey (small animals) by scenting the areas they have passed through.
Thanks to their night vision, they can see very well in the dark and move silently. Ocelots adapt to the environment while hunting their prey, then catch and capture their prey and drop it.
When a small group of living organisms (a small population) becomes separated from the main population (for example, when they move across a mountain range or river, or migrate to a new island where they cannot easily return), they often find themselves in a different environment than the one they originally came from.
This new environment has different resources and different competitors, so they require other characteristics or adaptations in order to become stronger competitors than previously demanded by the new popularity.
The population has remained absolutely unchanged; they are just as in need of adaptation as before.
Over time, the population begins to adapt to its new environment and becomes increasingly less similar to other populations.
Eventually, after thousands or even millions of years, two populations become so different in appearance that they can no longer be considered members of the same species.
We call this process speciation, which simply refers to the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome of evolution and a very important part of it.
