The origin of Taiwan dates back to the 15th century, when passing European sailors wrote down the island's name as Ilha Formosa or "Beautiful Island."
In 1624, the Dutch East India Company established a base in the southwestern part of Taiwan, initiating a transformation in local rice cultivation practices and recruiting Chinese workers to labor on rice and sugar plantations.
In 1683, the forces of the Sin dynasty (1644–1912) took control of the western and northern coastal regions of Taiwan, and in 1885, Taiwan was declared a province of the Sin Empire.
After suffering defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895), the Qing government signed the Treaty of Shimonoseki in 1895, under which it ceded control of Taiwan to Japan, which then governed the island until 1945.
