Just as Paris is regarded as the fashion capital of the modern world, Constantinople was also considered the fashion capital of feudal Europe.
Its fame as a cultural center began around 400 AD and lasted until approximately 1100 AD.
Its prestige declined in the 11th century, as those returning from the Byzantine market brought back more gifts than the silversmiths and spice merchants offered.
The transfer of the title "capital of fashion" from Constantinople to Paris occurred precisely at that time.
