A natural economy is an economy that is run solely to produce sufficient amounts of goods to meet the needs of the farmer and his family.
Supportive agriculture is a simple system, often organic, that maintains ecological balance by using leftover seeds from previous crops or employing other relatively simple techniques to increase yield.
Throughout history, many farmers have been engaged in subsidiary agriculture, and in many developing countries, this situation still persists today.
Subcultures unite like-minded individuals who feel ignored by social norms and whose self-awareness is enabled to develop.
