In essence, Tibetan Buddhism is deeply spiritual, consisting of Kundalini yoga, meditation, and the path of all-encompassing love.
In Kundalini Yoga, the Kundalini energy (the power of knowledge) is awakened through yoga postures, breathing exercises, mantras, and visualizations.
Tibetan meditation is considered a form of divine yoga. Through visualizing various deities, energy channels are purified, chakras are activated, and a state of enlightened consciousness is created.
Germany was the common enemy that led to cooperation between the USSR and the US during World War II. With the end of the war, the system, the process of development, and cultural clashes began to drive nations into conflict with one another.
Two years after the war ended, former allies became enemies, and the Cold War began.
It was to last another 40 years and involve real fighting carried out by proxy armies across a vast area stretching from Africa to Asia, including Afghanistan, Cuba, and many other battlefields.
By September 17, 1939, the Polish defense had already collapsed, and the only remaining hope was a retreat westward and reorganization along the Romanian frontier.
However, after the Soviet Union's Red Army, consisting of more than 800,000 soldiers, invaded Poland's eastern territories in violation of the Riga Peace Treaty, the Soviet-Polish Non-Aggression Pact, and other bilateral and multilateral agreements, its subsequent invasion of Belarus and Ukraine, along with the establishment of fronts, rendered these plans nearly obsolete overnight.
Using ships to transport goods is the most efficient method for moving large quantities of people and goods across oceans.
The task of naval forces, as a rule, is to maintain your nation's ability to move people and goods, while simultaneously hindering your rival's ability to move their own people and goods.
One of the most notable examples of this was the North Atlantic military operations during World War II. The Americans were attempting to transport people and supplies across the Atlantic Ocean to aid Britain.
At the same time, the German Navy was trying to stop this transport movement, mainly by using its submarines.
If the allies had not won, Germany could have conquered Britain just like the rest of Europe.
Sheep appear to have been first domesticated around 10,000 years ago in Iran's Zagros Mountains.
Ancient civilizations and tribes began preserving them for easy access to milk, wool, meat, and skins.
Sheep were traditionally kept in enclosures located on hills or other grazing areas, often tended by shepherds similar to the more familiar village shepherds, who were frequently children or young people. These traditional livestock practices are still used today.
Railway tracks were built in England around the 16th century.
Although the wagon roads consisted merely of parallel wooden planks, they enabled wagons to achieve greater speeds and to haul heavier loads than the slightly uneven roads of those days.
Railway tracks were discovered very early. However, it took a long time for people to realize that if iron were placed on top of them, railway tracks would become even more useful.
This became a common practice, but it led to increased wear on the wooden wheels of the rail cars.
Eventually, iron wheels replaced wooden wheels. In 1767, the first complete iron rails were produced.
The first known mode of transport was walking, which humans began when Homo Erectus (meaning "upright man") appeared about two million years ago.
Their ancestors, the Australopithecines, usually did not walk upright.
Although there is a possibility that Sahelanthropus walked on two legs seven million years ago, specialization in bipedal locomotion has been found in Australopithecus remains dating back 4.2–3.9 million years.
We can begin living in a more friendly way toward the environment, join environmental protection efforts, and even actively contribute to reducing future hardships to a certain extent.
This often resembles symptomatic treatment. But if we don't just want a temporary solution, we must identify the root causes of the problems and eliminate them.
It is evident to everyone that great changes have occurred in the world due to humanity's scientific and technological achievements, and that problems are becoming even greater because of the excessive growth of the population and humanity's extravagant lifestyle.
After being passed by Congress on July 4th, the handwritten copy signed by Congress President John Hancock and Clerk Charles Thomson was later sent to John Dunlap's print shop, located several blocks away.
During the era, between 150 and 200 copies were produced, which are now called "Danlep's side beards."
On July 8th, the document was read aloud to the public for the first time in the Independence Hall courtyard by John Nixon.
One of them was sent to George Washington on July 6th and read aloud to the troops in New York on July 9th. One copy arrived in London on August 10th.
The 25 surviving Dunlap plates that are still known to exist represent the oldest preserved copy of the document. The original manuscript has not survived.
Today, many paleontologists believe that a group of dinosaurs survived and are still alive today. We call them birds.
Many people do not consider them dinosaurs because they have feathers and can fly.
However, there are still many things resembling dinosaurs associated with birds.
They have feathers and claws on their feet, they lay eggs, and they walk on two hind legs like T-Rex.
All computers currently in use are based on information being encoded in binary digits.
Binary digits can take only one of two values, for example 0 or 1, and these digits are referred to as bits or binary digits in computer terminology.
Internal poisoning may not be immediately apparent. Symptoms such as nausea are general signs that may not allow for an immediate diagnosis.
Leaving medications or toxic chemicals used at home in open containers can be a good example of internal poisoning.
Check the special first aid instructions for this specific poison.
The term "Qo'ng'iz" is used officially by ethnologists for the mentioned group of insects.
This term originates from ancient familiarity with parasites highly adapted to living in humans.
Both biting and domestic mosquitoes have adapted to thrive in human dwellings or the homes of their hosts.
Approximately 400,000 cases of multiple sclerosis (MS) are known throughout the United States, remaining the leading neurological disease among younger and middle-aged adults.
MS is a condition that affects the central nervous system, including the brain, spinal cord, and optic nerves.
The study found that the likelihood of women acquiring MS was twice as high.
The parents may decide that having children is not in their own interest or in the interest of their children.
These couples might choose a plan for adoption for their children.
In adoption, the biological parents relinquish their parental rights, after which another couple can become the child's parents.
The main purpose of science is determining how the world functions through the scientific method. In essence, this method guides many other scientific researches.
However, this is not merely experiments and experience—it is a test used to exclude observations that could serve as a guide for possible one or more hypotheses, formulation of problems, and scientific research.
Naturalists and philosophers directed their attention toward sacred texts, especially the Gospel in Latin.
Aristotle's views on all scientific issues, including psychology, are accepted.
With the decline of knowledge in Greece, the West became detached from its Greek philosophical and scientific foundations.
Many rhythms observed in physiology and behavior are closely linked to the existence of endogenous cycles and their generation through biological clocks.
Periodic rhythms that are not simple responses to external periodic signals have been documented in many living organisms, including bacteria, yeast, plants, and animals.
Biological clocks are autogenerators that continue to maintain their rhythmic activity even in the absence of external signals.
One of the main hypotheses of the Hershey and Chase experiment was that DNA is genetic material.
Xershi and Cheyz used phages or viruses to transfer their DNA into bacteria.
They carried out both experiments, labeling the DNA in the phage with radioactive phosphorus and the protein in the phage with radioactive sulfur.
Mutations may have different effects depending on the type of mutation, the importance of the affected genetic material, and whether the affected cells are sex cells or not.
Only mutations in sex cells can be passed on to children; mutations in any other cells may lead to cell death or cancer.
Nature-based tourism attracts people who are interested in visiting natural areas for the purpose of enjoying nature, particularly wild plants and animals.
Examples of outdoor activities include hunting, fishing, photography, birdwatching, visiting gardens, and learning about ecotourism.
For example, visiting Borneo to photograph and study orangutans.
Every morning, people drive past small towns to go to work, and they meet with people whose workplaces are in the very places they've just passed through.
In this dynamic transportation network, each person is somehow connected to a personal car-based transportation system and supports it.
Science has now clearly shown that the current large-scale carbon economy has deprived the biosphere of the stable conditions that have supported human evolution for the past 2 million years.
Each person is a participant in society and uses transportation systems. Almost all people use transportation systems.
In developed countries, you will rarely witness complaints about the quality of drinking water or bridges collapsing.
Why do transport systems generate such complaints, why do they make errors every day? Are transport engineers simply incompetent? Or is there something more important?
Traffic flow refers to the study of individual drivers and vehicles' movements between two points and their interactions with each other.
Unfortunately, predicting the flow of traffic is difficult, because drivers' behaviors cannot be predicted with 100% accuracy.
Indeed, drivers tend to maintain a reasonably consistent speed; therefore, traffic flows are somewhat consistent and can be approximately described mathematically.
To better represent traffic flow, the following relationships were established among the three fundamental properties: (1) flow, (2) density, and (3) speed.
These circumstances assist in planning, designing, and operating road facilities.
Insects were the first organisms to take flight. Their ability to fly helped them escape predators more easily and find food and mates more efficiently.
The majority of beetles have the ability to fold their wings along the length of their bodies.
This provides them with larger, more spacious areas to hide from predators.
Among insects today, only dragonflies and mayflies are unable to fold their wings.
A man named Aristarchus said thousands of years ago that the solar system revolves around the Sun.
Some people thought it was true, but most were convinced of the opposite; namely, that the solar system, including the Sun (and even other stars), revolves around the Earth.
That makes sense, because the Earth seems not to be moving, doesn't it?
The Amazon is the largest and second-longest river on Earth. It carries eight times more water than the second-largest river.
Also, the Amazon is the widest river on Earth, reaching up to six miles in width in some places.
Twenty percent of the total water flowing from rivers into the oceans comes from the Amazon River.
The main Amazon River is 6,387 km (3,980 miles). It collects water from thousands of smaller rivers.
Although the construction of stone pyramids continued until the end of the ancient kingdom, they never surpassed the Giza pyramids in terms of their size and technical superiority.
The ancient Egyptians of the New Kingdom were amazed by the monuments of their ancestors, which were already over a thousand years old at that time.
The population of Vatican City is about 800 people. It is the smallest independent state and the least populous country in the world.
Vatican City uses the Italian language for legislation and official communication.
Although Latin is frequently used in religious ceremonies, Italian is considered the everyday language used by the majority of people working in government.
All the residents of Vatican City are Catholics.
Because all of these can be found naturally in nature and are relatively easy to extract using simple tools, humans have known about basic chemical elements such as gold, silver, and copper since ancient times.
Philosopher Aristotle advanced the theory that all things are composed of a combination of one or more of the four elements. He mentioned that these are earth, water, air, and fire.
It resembles the four states of matter (in the same order): solid, liquid, gas, and plasma, but it also proposed a theory about matter transforming into new forms that create the things we observe.
Alloys are primarily mixtures of two or more metals. Don't forget that there are many elements in the periodic table.
Elements like calcium and potassium are considered metals. Of course, metals such as silver and gold also exist.
Also, you can have alloys containing small amounts of non-metallic elements such as carbon.
Matter makes up everything in the universe. Tiny particles called atoms make up matter.
Atoms are so small that trillions of atoms can fit into the dot at the end of this sentence.
In this way, when the pen appeared, it became a good friend to many.
Unfortunately, due to the emergence of new writing methods, the pen has declined to a lower status and is used less frequently.
People are now writing directly on computer screens, so there's no need to get close to a pen or pencil.
When something new is created, you can only guess how the keyboard will look.
A nuclear fission bomb operates on the principle of energy release during the formation of a nucleus composed of many protons and neutrons.
It's like pushing a heavy car up a hill. With nuclear fission, again, part of that energy is released.
Some atoms have unstable nuclei, which means they are prone to splitting either with or without a small trigger.
The Moon's surface is made up of rocks and dust. The Moon's outer layer is called the crust.
The crust's thickness is 70 km on the Moon's visible side from Earth, and 100 km on the far side.
It is thinner under the Ocean Sea and thicker in mountainous areas.
There may be more maria nearby, as the crust is thinner there, making it easier for lava to reach the surface.
Content theories are focused on identifying things that influence people or attract them.
These theories indicate that humans have specific inner needs and/or desires before reaching adulthood.
These theories examine what motivates certain individuals to perform particular actions and how various factors in their environments influence whether they perform or refrain from certain actions.
Two well-known theories of motivation—Maslow's "Hierarchy of Needs" theory and Herzberg's "Two-Factor" theory.
Generally speaking, when managers start leading their former colleagues, two types of behavior may emerge. One end of the spectrum is trying to be one of the guys (or one of the girls).
These types of managers struggle with making unpopular decisions, enforcing discipline, evaluating work quality, assigning tasks, and holding people accountable.
At the other end of the spectrum, a person becomes an indistinguishable individual who believes that everything the group has done so far must be changed and made their own.
In the end, the leading team is responsible for both its success and failure.
Such behavior often leads to disagreements between leaders and other team members.
Virtual teams adhere to the same high standards of excellence as traditional teams, but there are subtle differences.
Virtual group members often serve as contact points for their physical groups.
Since their teams may meet across different time zones that might be difficult for local management to understand, they often have more autonomy than traditional teams.
The existence of a true "invisible team" (Larson and LaFasto, 1989, p. 109) is also a distinctive component of virtual teams.
"The invisible team" is a management team where each member is accountable. The invisible team establishes standards for each member.
Why would an educational organization desire to go through a time-consuming process of restructuring? One of the goals of implementing organizational educational concepts is innovation.
When all available resources in an organization's functional departments are used effectively, innovation and creativity can emerge.
As a result, an organization process that collaborates to overcome obstacles may initiate new innovative processes that serve customer needs.
Before an organization becomes innovative, its leadership must establish an innovation culture, as well as knowledge sharing and organizational training.
Anxel (2006) defines the interconnectedness approach as a method used to assist organizations in achieving a high level of effectiveness.
Neurobiological information provides empirical evidence for the theoretical approach to understanding intelligence. Therefore, it narrows down and makes the research field more precise.
The interconnection between brain pathology and behavior is supported by scientists' research and reinforces their viewpoints.
It has long been known that various brain injuries, traumas, diseases, and tumors can affect behavior and cause changes in certain brain activities.
The development of new technologies has provided us with the opportunity to observe and study the structure and functioning of the brain in ways that had never been seen before.
It provides us with a wealth of data and materials to create simulation models that help us understand the processes in our minds.
Although AI has a strong scientific fiction connotation, AI constitutes a very important field within computer science, dealing with behavior, machine learning, and intelligent adaptation.
Research in AI involves creating machines to automate tasks that require intelligent behavior.
Monitoring, planning, and scheduling, the ability to respond to customer inquiries and questions, as well as written, verbal, and facial recognition, can serve as examples of this.
Things aimed at solving such real-life problems have turned into separate sciences.
AI systems are now frequently used in fields such as finance, medicine, engineering, and military, just as they are integrated into many personal computers and video games.
Excursions are a major part of any lesson. In many cases, teachers would like to take their students to places where traveling by bus is impossible.
Technology offers solutions through virtual excursions to locations. Students can view artifacts in museums, visit an aquarium, or appreciate fine art, all while sitting in their classroom.
Conducting excursions virtually is an excellent way to discuss travel experiences and share insights with future lessons.
For example, students at Bennett School in North Carolina create a website each year about their trips to the state capital, the website is redesigned each year, but old versions are preserved online to serve as an online album.
Blogs also help students improve their writing skills. Although students often begin their blog experience with messy grammar and spelling, the presence of an audience usually motivates them to improve.
Since students are often the most critical listeners, bloggers strive to improve what they write in order to avoid criticism.
At the same time, blogging "compels students to become more knowledgeable about the world around them." The need to satisfy public interest motivates students to be insightful and interesting (Toto, 2004).
Blogging is a tool that inspires collaboration and encourages students to expand their knowledge beyond traditional classroom days.
Proper use of blogs enables students to become better analytical and critical thinkers; through active responses to Internet materials, students can define their position in the context of others' writings and also express their own viewpoints on specific issues. (Oravek, 2002)
Ottawa, Canada's beautiful bilingual capital, boasts a number of art galleries and museums reflecting Canada's history and present.
To the east is the Niagara Falls, and to the north are Muskoka and, beyond them, untouched natural beauty.
All of these, and others, emphasize how Ontarians are perceived by foreigners as typically Canadian.
In the north and northeast, the population of large areas is very sparse, and some places are almost uninhabited deserts.
When comparing populations, this surprises many: there are more African Americans than Canadian citizens living in the U.S.
The East African islands are located in the Indian Ocean along the eastern coast of the African continent.
When it comes to wild nature, Madagascar is the largest and most unique continent in the world.
Most of the smaller islands are independent states or associated with France and are famous as resort destinations with beautiful beaches.
The Arabs also brought Islam to these places, which became significant in the Comoros Islands and Mayotte.
Because the Portuguese explorer Vasco da Gama discovered the sea route from Europe to India, European influence and colonial policy began in the 15th century.
The northern region is bordered by the Sahel and by the Atlantic Ocean to the south and west.
Women: Regardless of their marital status, all female travelers are advised to identify themselves as married.
Wearing a ring is also beneficial (as long as it doesn't look too expensive, of course).
Women need to understand that cultural differences can lead to situations they might perceive as harassment, and they should remain vigilant, as such instances are not uncommon.
Be firm when saying no to men, and don't fear standing by your words (regardless of cultural differences, this won't make it any less straightforward!).
The city of Modern Cádiz was founded in the 10th century CE by Berber fishermen, and subsequently used as a strategic port by the Phoenicians, Romans, and Merenids, who named it Anfa.
The Portuguese rebuilt it under the name of Casca Branca after destroying it, and abandoned it after the 1755 earthquake.
The Sultan of Morocco rebuilt the city as Daru l-Badya and it was named Casablanca by Spanish traders who established trading posts there.
Casablanca is one of the most interesting places in all of Morocco for shopping.
It's easy to find places around Old Medina selling traditional Moroccan products such as tajines, clay vessels, charm accessories, kilims, and various decorations, but all of them are for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city located in the far east of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, near Rwanda.
In 2002, the lava flow from the Nyiragongo volcano destroyed the streets in the city of Goma, especially those in the city center, turning them into ashes.
Although Goma is sufficiently safe, any visits outside Goma should be carefully studied to understand the ongoing clashes frequently occurring in North Kivu province.
The city is also a base for climbing Nyiragongo Volcano, along with Africa's cheapest gorilla trekking.
You can use a boda-boda (motorcycle taxi) to tour around Goma. For short trips, the regular (local) fare is approximately 500 Congolese francs.
Along with its limited accessibility, "Timbuktu" began to be used as a metaphor for distant, exotic lands.
Today, despite its reputation and the presence of an airport, Timbuktu remains a modest little town.
Due to the danger of desert sands, in 1990 it was included in the list of World Cultural Heritage sites at risk.
This was one of the main stops in Henry Louis Gates Jr.'s PBS special series "The Wonders of the African World."
This city differs sharply from other cities in the country, as it is more Arab in style than African.
Kruger National Park (KNP) is located in northeastern South Africa, bordered by Mozambique to the east, Zimbabwe to the north, and the Limpopo River to the south.
The park covers an area of 19,500 km² and is divided into 14 ecological zones, each supporting different types of wildlife.
It is one of the main attractions in Southern Africa and is regarded as the flagship of South African National Parks (SANParks).
Scientists are currently working on creating a reactor capable of producing energy using the same method.
It might be beneficial to purchase a Wild Card, which grants access to selected parks in Southern Africa or all national parks in Southern Africa.
Hong Kong Island, which gave its name to the Hong Kong region, is a major destination attracting numerous tourists.
The cluster of buildings forming Hong Kong's skyline resembles a distinct, shiny striped pattern due to the presence of Victoria Harbour's waters.
To see Hong Kong's best view, come out from the island and go to the Kowloon waterfront located on the opposite side.
A large part of Hong Kong's urban development has taken place on reclaimed land along the northern shoreline.
This is the place that the British colonists considered their own, so if you're looking for evidence of the region's colonial past, this is a good place to start.
The Sundarbans, the world's largest coastal mangrove forest, stretches 80 km (50 miles) from the coastal region into the interior of Bangladesh and India.
Sundarban has been declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The part of the forest located in the Indian territory is called "Sundarbans National Park."
But forests are not just mangrove swamps—they also include the last remaining trees from vast grasslands that once covered the Indian subcontinent.
The Sundarbans cover an area of approximately 3,850 km², with about one-third of the region being covered by water/wetland areas.
Since 1966, Sandarban has been a wildlife sanctuary, and it is estimated that currently there are about 400 Bengal tigers and nearly 30,000 wild water buffalo in this region.
Buses depart throughout the day from the inter-district bus stop (on the opposite side of the river), but the majority, especially those going eastward and to Jakar/Bumtang, depart between 06:30 and 07:30.
Inter-city buses are often full, so it's recommended to buy tickets several days in advance.
Many districts are served by Japan's convenient and sturdy freight buses.
Companion taxis are considered fast and convenient ways to travel to places that are not too far away, such as Paro (Nu 150) and Punakha (Nu 200).
The Oyapok River bridge is a suspended bridge supported by cables. It is stretched along the Oyapok River, connecting the cities of Oyapok in Brazil and Saint-Georges-de-l'Oyapok in French Guiana.
The two minarets reach a height of 83 meters and have two walkways, each 378 meters long and 3.5 meters wide.
The vertical clearance under the bridge is 15 meters. Construction was completed in August 2011, but it remained closed to vehicle traffic until March 2017.
The bridge is scheduled to be fully operational in September 2017, when the construction of the Brazilian border control checkpoints is expected to be completed.
The Guarani are the most significant indigenous group currently residing in the eastern region of Paraguay and have traditionally lived as semi-nomadic hunters engaged in subsistence agriculture.
In the Chaco region, local tribes such as the Guaycuru and Payagua lived, sustaining themselves through hunting, fishing, and gathering.
Paraguay, which was formerly known as the "Great Province of the Indians," emerged in the 16th century as a result of conflicts between Spanish conquerors and local indigenous groups.
The Spanish began their colonial era, which lasted for three centuries.
Since its founding in 1537 as Asunción, Paraguay has succeeded in preserving much of its original characteristics and unique identity.
Argentina is one of the best polo teams in the world and is famous for its players.
In December, the largest tournament of the year will be held at the polo stadiums in Las Cañitas.
In other times of the year, smaller competitions and matches can also be seen here.
For information on where to purchase tickets for polo matches and the latest news about tournaments, contact the Argentina Polo Association.
The official currency of Folklend is the Folklend pound (FKP), whose value has been set equal to one British pound (GBP).
There is a currency exchange facility available at the only bank on the island located opposite the FIC West store in Stenlid.
British pounds are widely accepted throughout the islands, and both Sterling credit cards and US dollars are frequently accepted as well.
Although British and U.S. currencies may be accepted, credit cards might not be accepted in remote islands; confirm in advance with the owners which payment methods are accepted.
It is almost impossible to exchange Folklend currency outside the islands, so exchange your money before leaving the islands.
Because Montevideo is located south of the Equator, when it is winter in the Northern Hemisphere, it is summer there, and vice versa.
Montevideo is located in a subtropical zone; it is common for temperatures to exceed +30°C during summer months.
Winter can be chilling: temperatures rarely drop below freezing, but when wind and humidity combine, it may feel colder than what the thermometer indicates.
There are no distinct "rainy" and "dry" seasons: the amount of rainfall remains almost the same throughout the year.
Although most of the animals in the park are accustomed to seeing people, wild nature is undoubtedly wild, and they should neither be fed nor disturbed.
According to the recreation park authorities, stay at least 100 yards/meters away from bears and wolves, and at least 25 yards/meters away from all other wild animals!
Bison, elk, moose, bears, and nearly all large animals have the potential to attack, no matter how calm they may appear.
Every year, dozens of visitors get injured because they fail to maintain a sufficient distance. These animals can be aggressive, wild, and dangerous, so please give them their own territory.
Also, odors from food attract bears and other wild animals, so avoid carrying or cooking fragrant foods and keep your camp clean.
Apia is the capital of Samoa. The city is located on the island of Upolu and has fewer than 40,000 residents.
Founded in the 1850s, Apia has been the official capital of Samoa since 1959.
In 1889, when Germany, the U.S., and Britain rejected the departure of nine ships from the port, the port became a hotspot for intense maritime confrontation.
All the ships except one British cruiser had been sunk. Over 200 Americans and Germans were killed.
The struggle for independence organized by the Mau movement led to the killing of the high chief Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III during a peaceful protest in the city.
Because Auckland is surrounded by two harbors, it has numerous beaches. The most famous ones are located in three areas.
The Northern Coast beaches, located in the Northern Harbors district, stretch along the Pacific Ocean from the northern Long Bay to the southern Devonport.
They are sandy beaches, almost like swimming-safe, and mostly shaded by palm trees.
Tamaki Drive beaches are located on the Waitemata Harbour, along the prestigious coastlines of the cities of Mission Bay and Saint Heliers in Central Auckland.
These are family beaches with rows of shops along the shore, which at times can get crowded. Swimming is safe.
The main local beer is called "Number One," which is not a complex-style beer, but rather tasty and refreshing. The name of the second local beer is "Manta."
There are many French wines available to drink, but wines from New Zealand and Australia might be doing better on the market.
Local tap water is completely safe, but if you're concerned, it's easy to find bottled water.
The concept of "flat white" coffee is unfamiliar to Australians. "Espresso" is called "black coffee," cappuccino is served with frothed milk (not whipped cream), and tea is served without milk.
Hot chocolate meets Belgian standards. Fruit juices, though expensive, are excellent.
